Q: Why doesn&#39;t C# support local static variables like C does? A: <p>I don't see much added benefit to local statics. If you are keeping your classes single purpose and small, there is little problem with global static pollution as the naysayers like to complain about. But here is just one other alternative.</p>
<pre><code>using System;
using System.Collections;

public class Program
{
    delegate bool DoWork();

    public static void Main()
    {
        DoWork work = Foo().GetEnumerator().MoveNext;

        work();
        work();
        work();
    }

    public static IEnumerable Foo()
    {
        int static_x = 10;
        /*
           Do some other static stuff....
           */

         main:

         // Repetative housework
         Console.WriteLine(static_x);
         static_x++;

         yield return true;
         goto main;
     }
 }
</code></pre>

Q: How to ignore global cache on some routes in NestJs A: <p>Just an alternative to the solution provided by <strong>NoncomissionedRush</strong>. It could also be done using a decorator, for example <code>@NoCache()</code>. It could be implemented like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>export const NO_CACHE_KEY = 'noCache';

export const NoCache = () =&gt; SetMetadata(NO_CACHE_KEY, true);
</code></pre>
<p>Then, override <code>isRequestCacheable</code> but while using the decorator metadata. If not present, simply invoke the behavior of the superclass. In my case I provided an alias for the default interceptor.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>@Injectable()
export class CacheInterceptor extends DefaultCacheInterceptor {
  isRequestCacheable(context: ExecutionContext) {
    const noCache = this.reflector.getAllAndOverride&lt;boolean&gt;(NO_CACHE_KEY, [
      context.getHandler(),
      context.getClass(),
    ]);

    if (noCache) return false;

    return super.isRequestCacheable(context);
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>With this approach, you may define the routes you don't want cached by using the decorator over them, instead of having a hardcoded list. Just a different solution.</p>

Q: Is there anything like pointers in C#? A: <p>C# has <em><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_Sharp_%28programming_language%29#Categories_of_data_types" rel="nofollow noreferrer">references</a></em> which are very similar to pointers.  If <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> are both references to the same object, a change in <code>a</code> will also be seen in <code>b</code>.</p>
<p>For example, in:</p>
<pre><code>class X {
    public int val;
}

void Main()
{
    var a = new X();
    var b = a;
    a.val = 6;
    Console.WriteLine(b.val);
}
</code></pre>
<p><code>6</code> will be written.</p>
<p>If you change the declaration of <code>X</code> from <code>class</code> to <code>struct</code>, then <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> will no longer be references, and <code>0</code> will be written.</p>
